2026-10-07 17:12 t/113 diagnosed on Windows 11, Strawberry Perl 5.42.0, tree 319aef8 (windows-developer k19). Two things stack up; neither is `_run_git`.

1. With PATH emptied karr cannot start on Strawberry at all. The DLLs that FFI::Platypus and libgit2 depend on live in `C:\Strawberry\c\bin` and are found through PATH:

    PATH=''                      perl -MFFI::Platypus -e1  -> exit 2, "Can't load '.../auto/FFI/Platypus/Platypus.xs.dll' ... load_file: module not found"
    PATH='C:\Strawberry\c\bin'   perl -MFFI::Platypus -e1  -> exit 0
    PATH='C:\Strawberry\c\bin'   perl -Ilib bin/karr --version -> karr 0.603, 0.5s

   `bin/karr init` under the empty PATH exits 2 at once with that message plus the "Compilation failed in require" chain, about 1.1 KB on stderr. The in-process subtest before it ("the index answers anyway") passes only because the DLLs are already loaded.

2. The test then deadlocks on its own read order: it reads the child's stdout to EOF before touching stderr. A pipe made by perl on Win32 holds 512 bytes. Same open3 pattern, child = `perl -e "print STDERR 'x' x N; exit 2"`:

    N=400  exit=2, read 400      N=512  exit=2, read 512
    N=513  hangs                 N=1100, 4096, 4097  hang

   So the child blocks writing byte 513 of the error, the parent blocks waiting for stdout EOF. Killing the child does not free the parent in the cpanm run because the test never reaches waitpid.

Fix candidates for the test: on MSWin32 build the "no git" PATH from the directories perl's DLLs need (`C:\Strawberry\c\bin`, i.e. $Config{bin}-relative) rather than '', and send the child's stderr to a file or merge it into stdout (`2>&1` handle) instead of a second pipe read after EOF. The same read-stdout-then-stderr pattern is worth a grep across t/ -- any child that says more than 512 bytes on stderr hangs its test on Windows.

